<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Brimstone Hill Fortress</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Brimstone Hill Fortress is a British fortress on a steep volcanic hill on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a>, built mainly between 1690 and 1790 by enslaved African labourers. Known as the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>', it fell to a French siege in 1782 but was returned under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1783">Treaty of Paris</a> in 1783. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/brimstone-hill-fortress.jpg" alt="Brimstone Hill Fortress" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ukexpat · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BrimstoneHill01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The fortifications stand on Brimstone Hill, a very steeply sloping hill close to the sea on the western, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast of St Kitts. They were designed by British military engineers and built and maintained by enslaved Africans. Cannon were first mounted on the hill in 1689, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-baptiste-du-casse">Jean-Baptiste du Casse</a> used them to capture the English Fort Charles. A year later the English under Sir Christopher Codrington used the same tactic to recapture it. From then on the English used the hill as a fortress, mounting 24-pound <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> and taking advantage of its height of 972 feet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Lightning destroyed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> magazine in 1711 and again in 1731, and by 1736 the fort had 49 guns. From 1795, 40 members of the St Christopher Corps of Embodied Slaves served at the fort, armed with pikes and cutlasses. By 1780 it was considered impregnable. On 11 January 1782 the French under Admiral Comte François Joseph Paul de Grasse and François Claude Amour, marquis de Bouillé, laid siege to it. During the siege the neighbouring island of Nevis surrendered, and guns from Fort Charles and other small forts there were brought to St Kitts for use against the hill. British Admiral Hood could not dislodge de Grasse, and after a month the heavily outnumbered and cut-off garrison surrendered.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1783">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1783 restored St Kitts, Brimstone Hill and Nevis to British rule. The British then strengthened the fortifications, and the hill never again fell to an enemy. The French navy failed to recapture it in 1806, though Admiral Édouard Thomas Burgues de Missiessy had blown up the powder magazine in a raid in 1805.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Local militias throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> were disbanded in 1838 after the Napoleonic Wars. The British abandoned the fort in 1853 and disbanded its militia in 1854, and the structures gradually decayed through vandalism and natural processes. Stabilisation and restoration began in the early 1900s. In 1973 Prince Charles reopened the first fully restored area, the Prince of Wales Bastion. Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> unveiled a plaque naming it a national park in 1985, legislation made it one in 1987, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> declared it a World Heritage Site in 1999. Visitors can see the Fort George Citadel and its museum, the Western and Eastern Place of Arms and the Magazine Bastion that the French breached in 1782.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Brimstone Hill Fortress National Park” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The French captured <b>Brimstone Hill</b> in 1782 but returned it in 1783.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-saint-christopher">Colony of Saint Christopher</a></li></ul></section>
